# nanDECK design notes

## Card photo framing

Card photos use proportional cover scaling to fill the photo frame. The frame
matches the 2.5-inch cut-line width, from x=0.12 to x=2.62, and extends to the
top edge. Portrait photos fill the printed card width and are cropped at the
top and bottom. The template reads EXIF orientation before cropping. Keep
important subjects near the center because cover scaling crops excess image
area.

## Adding a glyph

The card template embeds glyphs inside `HTMLTEXT` fields. Adding a glyph requires all of the following:

1. Create the source vector at `design/glyphs/<name>.svg`.
   - Use lowercase `snake_case` for `<name>`.
   - Keep the artwork simple, monochrome black/grey, and on a transparent background.
   - Use a `viewBox="0 0 120 120"` so glyphs have consistent proportions.

2. Run the rasterizer from the repository root. It requires Node.js and the `sharp` package; check that the package is available first:

   ```powershell
   node -e "require('sharp'); console.log('sharp available')"
   node design/rasterize_glyphs.js
   ```

   The script scans every SVG in `design/glyphs`, recreates every corresponding transparent 240×240 PNG in `design/glyphs/png`, and intentionally does extra work for unchanged glyphs. `HTMLIMAGE` should point to the PNG; SVG files are kept as the editable source but are not the reliable inline-image format for this template.

   If the check reports that `sharp` cannot be found, do not claim that PNG regeneration succeeded and do not proceed as though the PNGs were updated. In a Codex workspace, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to obtain the configured Node.js executable and Node.js packages directory, then run the script with that runtime and set `NODE_PATH` to its `node_modules` directory:

   ```powershell
   $env:NODE_PATH = "<configured Node.js packages>"
   & "<configured Node.js executable>" design/rasterize_glyphs.js
   Remove-Item Env:NODE_PATH
   ```

   The run is successful only when the script prints `Rasterized N glyphs into ...`. The script loads `sharp` before writing any files, so a missing dependency leaves the existing PNGs untouched.

3. Register the glyph in `design/the_nos_card_template.nde`, before the `LINK` directive:

   ```nde
   HTMLIMAGE = , "(<name>)", "glyphs/png/<name>.png", 0.11, 0.11, PB
   ```

   The parenthesized key is the exact marker that goes into card data. Keep the `PB` flags. Do not add `H`: in the Explorer HTML renderer, `H` suppresses spacing and can make adjacent glyphs overlap.

4. Put the marker into a CSV text field, normally `Effect` or `Flavor`:

   ```csv
   "Move (arrow_left) two spaces, then (arrow_right) one space."
   ```

   The template uses `HTMLTEXT` for those fields, with `HTMLFONT` definitions and the `ET` renderer flags. Do not change them to `TEXT`, or glyph markers will print as ordinary text. Do not switch to the `K` renderer unless Chrome rendering has been configured and tested.

5. Add the new marker to the Campfire showcase row in `design/the_nos_card_template_data.csv` so every glyph can be reviewed together. Preserve the existing showcase markers; append the new one rather than replacing them.

6. Validate the change:
   - Confirm both the SVG and PNG exist.
   - Confirm the `HTMLIMAGE` key, PNG path, and CSV marker use the same `<name>`.
   - Confirm the CSV still loads with `Import-Csv`.
   - Run `git diff --check` for the changed design files.
   - Rebuild/refresh the `.nde` file in nanDECK and check the showcase card. The workspace may not have nanDECK installed, so textual validation alone is not a rendering check.

The current inline keys are `(arrow_left)`, `(arrow_right)`, `(boardgame)`, `(card)`, `(energy)`, `(exercise)`, `(food)`, `(fun)`, `(indoors)`, `(item)`, `(relax)`, `(social)`, `(suitcase)`, `(tree)`, and `(trunk)`.
